      },
      "op": "replace_item_field",
      "page_id": "comodules-matrix-coefficients-and-coalgebra-duality",
      "item_id": "lem-hh-every-comodule-element-lies-in-a-finite-subcomodule",
      "field": "statement",
      "before": "Assuming AC, every element of every right comodule over a k-coalgebra is contained in a finite-dimensional subcomodule. If C is finite-dimensional, the proof has a separate choice-free branch.",
      "value": "Assume AC. Let $C$ be a coalgebra over a field $k$, let $(M,\\rho)$ be a right $C$-comodule, and let $m\\in M$. Then $m$ belongs to a finite-dimensional subcomodule of $M$. If $C$ is finite-dimensional, the same conclusion follows without AC."
    },
    {
      "target_path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-2.pages.json",
      "guard": {
        "path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-2.pages.json",
        "sha256": "725724f7d427ee356929d436f3f4e978f447b2654b9fa4dc0af10b577f3de444"
      },
      "op": "replace_item_field",
      "page_id": "comodules-matrix-coefficients-and-coalgebra-duality",
      "item_id": "thm-hh-comodules-and-rational-dual-modules",
      "field": "statement",
      "before": "Assuming AC, right C-comodules and their maps correspond to rational left C*-modules and their maps. For finite-dimensional C every left C*-module is rational by a choice-free finite-dual argument; no assertion is made that every module is rational for arbitrary C.",
      "value": "Assume AC and let $C$ be a coalgebra over a field $k$. The assignment\n$$ (M,\\rho)\\longmapsto \\bigl(M,\\ f\\cdot m:=(\\operatorname{id}_M\\otimes f)\\rho(m)\\bigr) $$\nidentifies right $C$-comodules and their maps with rational left $C^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_k(C,k)$-modules and their maps. The finite-tensor representative defining the coaction of a rational module is unique. If $C$ is finite-dimensional, every left $C^*$-module is rational and the full correspondence is choice-free. The forward comodule-to-module construction is choice-free for every $C$."
    },
    {
      "target_path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-5.pages.json",
      "guard": {
        "path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-5.pages.json",
        "sha256": "14b9ed462a1382da87c48f4f8550f387490424b3398a569e95fb7b3ee7eca662"
      },
      "op": "replace_item_field",
      "page_id": "hopf-module-tensor-products-and-rigid-duality",
      "item_id": "thm-hh-left-dual-evaluation-coevaluation-and-triangle-identities",
      "field": "statement",
      "value": "Let $H$ be a bialgebra over a field $k$ with antipode $S$, meaning a linear map satisfying\n\n$$\\sum S(h_{(1)})h_{(2)}=\\varepsilon(h)1_H=\\sum h_{(1)}S(h_{(2)}).$$\n\nLet $V$ be a finite-dimensional left $H$-module and give $V^*$ the candidate action and maps of [[def-hh-left-dual-of-a-finite-hopf-module]]. Then $S(ab)=S(b)S(a)$ and $S(1_H)=1_H$; the candidate action makes $V^*$ a left $H$-module; evaluation $V^*\\otimes V\\to k$ and coevaluation $k\\to V\\otimes V^*$ are $H$-linear; and the two triangle composites are identities. No bijectivity of $S$ is assumed."
    },
    {
      "target_path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-7.pages.json",
      "guard": {
        "path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-7.pages.json",
        "sha256": "23e4adf3076c87f74e3e36afd9195820e15b501e46cb28d9552f47d5a489fa87"
      },
      "op": "replace_item_field",
      "page_id": "finite-hopf-integrals-frobenius-duality-and-maschke",
      "item_id": "thm-hh-hopf-maschke-integral-averaging",
      "field": "statement",
      "value": "Let $H\\ne0$ be a finite-dimensional Hopf algebra as in [[def-hh-hopf-algebra-and-antipode]] over a field $k$. Then $H$ is semisimple as a ring ([[def-semisimple-ring]]), that is, its left regular module is a direct sum of simple left submodules ([[def-semisimple-module]]), if and only if there exists a left integral $\\Lambda\\in H$ with $\\varepsilon(\\Lambda)\\ne0$. In that case $\\Lambda$ can be normalized so that $\\varepsilon(\\Lambda)=1$."
    },
    {
      "target_path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-7.pages.json",
      "guard": {
        "path": "research/hopf-hecke-unfinished-15-2026-10-15-batch-7.pages.json",
        "sha256": "23e4adf3076c87f74e3e36afd9195820e15b501e46cb28d9552f47d5a489fa87"
      },
      "op": "replace_item_field",
      "page_id": "finite-hopf-integrals-frobenius-duality-and-maschke",
      "item_id": "thm-hh-finite-integrals-are-one-dimensional-and-antipode-is-bijective",
      "field": "statement",
      "value": "Let $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional Hopf algebra over a field $k$. Each of $\\int_H^\\ell$, $\\int_H^r$, $\\int_{H^*}^\\ell$, and $\\int_{H^*}^r$ is one-dimensional. The antipodes of $H$ and $H^*$ are bijective; each sends the left-integral space isomorphically onto the right-integral space."
    }
  ],
